Topic 2. 3.1 #2 Network Infrastructure Concepts
This one is about separating networks so that one problem can’t spread everywhere. 
Think of it as ways to divide up a building:

Physical isolation (air gap). A completely separate building with no road connecting it. No cables, no wireless, nothing. It’s used for highly sensitive
systems like power plant controls or military networks. It’s very secure but inconvenient, because moving data in or out means physically carrying it,
often on a USB stick, which brings its own risks.

Logical segmentation (VLANs). One building divided into separate locked flats. It’s the same physical switch and cabling, but software keeps the groups apart,
for example Finance in one VLAN and Guest Wi-Fi in another. It’s cheaper and more flexible than separate hardware. 

SDN (Software-Defined Networking). This splits the network into three layers, like a road system:
Data plane: the cars on the road actually moving (traffic being forwarded).
Control plane: the traffic lights and road rules deciding which way traffic goes (routing tables, decisions). Tells you when you can do a U turn or its 40kph. 
Management plane: The BOSS. The council office where someone sets up and changes the rules(admin access and configuration). This is the control centre it tells
us what we can do and decide if the pot holes get filled or not(council makes the decisions often waits till the damn potholes have potholes. 

The security point is that the management plane is the crown jewels. Whoever controls the council office controls every road, so admin access there must be tightly
locked down.
