Topic 3. 3.1 #3, Other Infrastructure Concepts
Where things live and who’s in charge:
On-premises. Everything is in your own building. You have full control, but you also have full responsibility: physical security, power, patching, the lot. It’s owning
yoar car outright. Centralised vs decentralised. Centralised means one head office controls everything. It’s easy to manage and monitor, but 
it’s a single point of failure. Decentralised spreads control across many locations, so it’s more resilient but harder to keep consistent. Think of one big hospital vs
a network of local clinics.

Ways to run software:
Virtualisation. One physical computer runs several full virtual machines, each with its own complete operating system. Picture separate houses on one block of land. 
Wrote up VM escape from 2.3. Containers. Lighter than VMs. Containers share the host’s operating system but keep each app in its own box. Picture flats in 
one apartment building: they share the foundations and plumbing, so if the shared base has a flaw, every flat is exposed.

Special devices that are hard to secure:
IoT. Smart devices like cameras, smart TVs and fridges. They often ship with weak default passwords and rarely get updates. That’s why they belong on a separate network,
like a guest Wi-Fi plan. SCADA / ICS. The systems that run power stations, water treatment and factories. Breaches here can cause physical harm, which is why they’re 
the classic air-gap case. RTOS (Real-Time Operating System). Systems that must respond instantly, such as car braking systems or medical equipment. 
You can’t pause them for a security scan or a reboot, so security must be built in from the start. Embedded systems. Computers built into a device to do one job,
like a pacemaker or a smart meter. They’re often impossible to patch, so the security has to be right before they leave the factory.

High availability:
Keeping systems running even when something fails, using backup hardware that takes over automatically. Think of a hospital’s backup generator. The trade-off is cost,
because you’re paying for spares you hope never to use. 


What’s the difference between a virtual machine and a container, and why does sharing the operating system matter for security?
Containers share the host’s kernel (the core of the operating system), so their isolation is weaker. If there’s a flaw in that shared kernel, or an attacker breaks out
of one container (a container escape), every container on that host is exposed. Go back to the flats: they all share one set of foundations, so a crack in the foundations
affects every flat.

VMs have stronger isolation. Each one has its own complete operating system, so if your Kali VM gets compromised, the attacker still has to break through the hypervisor
(VM escape, from 2.3) to reach your Windows host or other VMs. Separate houses on one block are harder to get between.

So in short:
VMs: more operating systems to manage, but stronger walls between them
Containers: lighter and easier to manage, but they share the same foundations

Why are IoT and embedded systems (like an MRI machine or a smart camera) so hard to secure?
More devices means a bigger attack surface. Every doorbell and camera is another door.
You rely on the manufacturer to patch it. If they stop releasing updates, you’re stuck with the flaws.
Mixed manufacturers and ages make it hard to manage everything consistently.
Keep only what you need. That’s a real security principle called minimising the attack surface. It’s the same thinking as decommissioning and hardening from 2.5.
Three more points to add:

Weak default passwords. Many devices ship with the same default login, and people never change it. The Mirai botnet took over hundreds of thousands of cameras and routers 
this way and used them for huge DDoS attacks. Long lifespans. A camera or MRI machine may run for 10+ years, long after the manufacturer stops supporting it.
Medical devices often can’t be patched freely. An MRI’s software is approved as part of the machine, so changing it can mean losing that approval. That’s why you see old
Windows versions still running medical equipment.

What is SCADA/ICS? SCADA (Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition) is a type of ICS (Industrial Control System). It’s the central control room that monitors 
and runs equipment spread over a wide area, like a water network, power grid or gas pipeline. ICS is the broader umbrella that also covers control systems inside a 
single factory.
Why would a breach of a SCADA system be more serious than a breach of a normal office network?  the output is physical, not digital.
On an office network, the worst case is: data leaked, files encrypted, systems down for a few days. People are annoyed. Money is lost. The building still has power,
the water still flows, the hospital still runs.
On a SCADA system, the worst case is:
A valve opens that shouldn't → chemical release, explosion, toxic spill
A pump stops that shouldn't → hospital loses power, water treatment stops, city goes dark
A reactor's cooling is disrupted → meltdown
A centrifuge spins out of control → physical destruction (STUXNET, 2010)
A dam gate opens → flooding downstream
The CIA triad inverts. In an office, confidentiality is king — a data leak is the headline. In SCADA, availability and integrity are king.
If the grid goes down, nobody cares whether your email was leaked — people lose power, surgeries stop, water stops being treated. If the integrity of a 
control signal is broken (the system tells the valve to open when it should be closed), people die.
The key difference is that an office breach loses data and money, while a SCADA breach can cause physical harm and cost lives.
A hospital is a strong example of the knock-on effects: water or power goes down, and suddenly an operating theatre can’t function.

Real examples worth knowing:
Stuxnet (around 2010): malware that physically damaged Iran’s nuclear centrifuges by making them spin out of control while showing operators normal readings.
Ukraine power grid (2015): attackers cut power to hundreds of thousands of people in winter.
Oldsmar, Florida (2021): someone accessed a water treatment plant remotely and tried to raise a chemical to dangerous levels. An operator spotted the mouse
moving on his screen and reversed it.

Why SCADA is so hard to secure:
It was designed for reliability, not security. Many systems are decades old and use protocols with no authentication.
It can’t just be switched off to patch. You can’t reboot a water network on a Tuesday afternoon.
That’s why it’s the classic case for air gaps and strict segmentation from 3.1 #2.

An Australian angle for cyber roles: critical infrastructure here is covered by the SOCI Act (Security of Critical Infrastructure Act), which puts security 
obligations on operators of things like energy, water and hospitals. As I have an interest in governance it would be good to look into. 
What’s an RTOS? why can’t you treat it like a normal computer when it comes to security? RTOS (Real-Time Operating System) is an operating system designed to
guarantee that a task completes within a strict, known deadline — not "as fast as possible," but by this specific time, every time, without exception.
A normal OS (Windows, Linux, macOS) is non-deterministic: it schedules tasks based on priority and available resources, and a task might get its turn in 2 ms or 200 ms.
That's fine for a web browser. It's unacceptable for:
                An airbag controller that must deploy within 30 ms of impact
                A car's anti-lock braking system that must modulate pressure every 10 ms
                A SCADA controller that must read a sensor and adjust a valve within 5 ms
                The RTOS scheduler is deterministic: it knows exactly which task runs when, and it guarantees that the highest-priority task always preempts everything
else immediately. No garbage collection pauses. No page faults. No "the OS is updating itself in the background."
How It Differs From a Normal OS?
Anything that could delay the task, like antivirus scans, heavy encryption or a reboot for an update, risks missing the deadline, and missing the deadline is the failure. 
For the exam: it’s not that RTOS devices have no security. It’s that security has to be handled differently:
 Put in at design time, kept lightweight, so it never gets in the way of the deadline
 Protected from the outside rather than from the inside: segment it from other networks, limit who can physically access it, and keep it off the internet where possible
 Patched carefully and rarely, because changes have to be tested to prove they don’t affect timing, a bit like the Medical Imaging PC approval issue.
What does high availability mean?
High availability (HA) is a system's ability to stay up and accessible even when individual components fail. The goal: the user never notices (or barely notices) a failure.
something fails and users never notice. How it’s actually done:
Redundancy. Have more than one of everything that matters: servers, power supplies, network links, even whole data centres.
Failover. When one fails, the backup takes over automatically. Think of the hospital generator kicking in when the mains power drops.
“Five nines.” Availability is often measured as a percentage. 99.999% uptime works out to only about five minutes of downtime a year. Each extra nine costs a lot more money.
The trade-off is cost. You’re paying for spare equipment you hope never to use, so companies weigh how much downtime would cost them against how much redundancy costs. 
A hospital or bank will pay for it; a small café’s website probably won’t. This links back to the A in the CIA triad from Domain 1. High availability is how you protect 
availability.

My smart doorbell lesson (IoT in real life)
A few years ago I bought a Ring doorbell. This is when all these devices were just comming out on the market and still new. I could never get it working properly after
I moved house with my newer iPhone, so I replaced it with a Google doorbell.  Then I found I couldn’t view it, so I bought a Google smart display to go with it, which was
not cheap at the time. It worked well for a while, and then the problems started. I had trouble mounting it, it fell off, and the lens got scratched. The battery kept going
flat, so I was constantly recharging it, and I think sitting in the sun is what finally killed the battery for good. Once the doorbell died, I stopped using the screen too, 
I had just got beautifull old french bedside tables that were really small. It now sits in a draw unused. The screen was taking up space, electricity and because the doorbell
was the main reason I had bought it. Looking back, it was nice to have, while it worked. One device led to another, and I ended up spending a lot on a door bell that my dog
does for free.

What I learned:
Do the research first. When these doorbells first came out, the boxes said almost nothing about compatibility, and because they were new, there wasn’t much online either.
I should have waited until other people had tried them. That’s what I do now. One device leads to another. IoT devices are often designed to work best inside one company’s
ecosystem, so buying one can lock you into buying more. Hardware fails. Batteries, weather, mounting problems and physical damage are all part of the real cost.

The security side (Security+ 3.1):
My doorbell story is a small version of a big problem businesses face with IoT:
Every device is another door. The more devices you add, the bigger your attack surface, and the more you have to secure, monitor and update.
You rely on the manufacturer. IoT devices only get patched if the company keeps releasing updates. Many stop long before the device stops working.
Limited power means limited security. Small batteries and processors often can’t run proper encryption, logging or security software.
Mixed brands and ages make it hard to manage everything consistently.

The question every business (and household) should ask is: do we actually need this device, or is it just convenient? Removing what you don’t need is one of the simplest
security wins there is. In Security+ it’s called minimising the attack surface.

THE BEST DOOR BELL IN THE WORLD. My other security system!!!
My dog is my favourite security system. He hears someone at the door long before any doorbell would, and he lets me know about it. He never needs a firmware update,
his battery never goes flat in the sun, there’s no app to stop working, and he’s never had a compatibility problem with my phone. He’s a security system, a companion, a foot
warmer, personal training companion all in one. He has multiple settings too. If its a stranger its a loud continuous bark at the door. If its a sound like a car door opening its usually 
one bark followed by quiet and then maybe a follow up bark. If it's some thing else that is unusual his hair stands up on the back of his back (like a rodesian ridgeback).
If its someone he knows its a cry. If its a person he knows well its a sudden rush to the door a long continuous cry that wont stop for ages or untill the door is finally 
opened.  

Not every problem needs a tech solution. Sometimes the simplest option is the most reliable one. My dog has never let me down and the best security device I have ever
owned. 

