Topic 4. 3.1 #4 Infrastructure Considerations.
The factors a business weighs when choosing between them. There’s no single right answer, only trade-offs.
choosing a car for a business:
Keeping it running:
Availability. Is it there when you need it? (You’ve just covered this.)
Resilience. How well does it cope when something goes wrong, and how quickly does it bounce back? Availability is about staying up; resilience is about recovering.
Ease of recovery. If it breaks down, how quickly can it be fixed? A common model gets parts the next day; a rare imported one might take weeks.

Cost and growth:
Cost. Not just the purchase price, but running costs, maintenance and staff time. My doorbell story is a perfect example of hidden costs.
Scalability. Can it grow with you? Cloud scales easily, like hiring extra cars when needed. On-prem means buying more hardware.
Responsiveness. How quickly it responds to users. Distance and load matter here.
Ease of deployment. How quick and simple it is to set up. IaC helps a lot here.

Security and risk: Risk transference. Shifting some risk to someone else, like buying insurance or using a cloud provider who takes on part of the responsibility. 
The risk doesn’t vanish; it moves. Patch availability. Will the manufacturer keep releasing updates?
Inability to patch. Some systems can’t be patched (embedded, RTOS, medical devices), so you need other protections like segmentation.

Physical limits: Power. Data centres and devices need reliable power, and backup power for high availability.
Compute. How much processing power is available. Small IoT devices have very little, which limits the security they can run.

What’s the difference between availability and resilience?
Availability is a state: is the system up right now, and for what percentage of the time? It's measured in nines, in uptime, in "the service responded."
Resilience is a capability: when the system gets hit, does it degrade gracefully, adapt, and recover — or does it collapse entirely and take hours to come back?
The difference in one sentence: availability asks "is it working?" Resilience asks "what happens when it's attacked, and how fast does it get back to working?"
Availability asks “is it up right now, and how often is it up?” Resilience asks “when it breaks, how fast does it bounce back?”
One term to add: MTTR (Mean Time To Repair), the average time it takes to get a system working again after a failure. Lower MTTR means more resilience.

In car terms:
Availability: the car is in the driveway and starts when you need it
Resilience: you get a flat tyre, but there’s a spare in the boot and you’re back on the road in 15 minutes
The two are connected, since a resilient system recovers quickly and so stays available more of the time.

What does risk transference mean, and does transferring the risk make it disappear? 
Risk transference is shifting the financial or operational impact of a risk to a third party.
You don't eliminate the risk, you don't reduce it, you don't accept it — you make someone else pay the bill when it happens like cyber insurance.
Risk transference is buying someone else to hold the bill when things go wrong. The risk doesn't disappear — the breach still happens, the outage still
occurs, the data still leaks. You just don't carry the full financial weight of it. It's the only one of the four risk responses that doesn't change the risk itself;
it only changes who pays. The risk doesn’t disappear; it just moves who pays and who helps fix it. Cyber insurance is the classic exam example.

Two things the insurance doesn’t transfer, which is a good point to make in interviews:

Reputation. Customers blame the company, not the insurer. Optus and Medibank both found that out in 2022.
Legal responsibility. Under Australia’s Notifiable Data Breaches scheme, the company still has to notify affected people and the regulator, however much is insured.
Also worth knowing: insurers now often require controls like MFA and tested backups before they’ll cover you. So transferring risk usually means reducing it as well.
Other examples of risk transference: outsourcing to a cloud provider (part of the shared responsibility model) or hiring a managed security provider to monitor your network.
The Notifiable Data Breaches scheme only requires companies to notify people when a breach is likely to result in serious harm, and the company makes that call itself.
So plenty of breaches never reach individual customers. It also only requires notification, not compensation. I have checked haveibeenpwned.com shows which known breaches. 
This is the reason I have a few different email addresses and use burner email addresses to avoid the junk mail.
What does scalability mean, and why is it easier in the cloud than on-premises?
Scalability is a system's ability to handle more load (more users, more data, more requests) without degrading, by adding more resources — and ideally, to handle
less load by removing them. Two directions: Scale up or scale accross. Scalability is "handle more (or less) load without breaking." On-prem, it's a capital 
project — order, ship, rack, install, configure, wait weeks. In the cloud, it's an API call — launch, configure, serve, terminate, pay per second. 
The cloud doesn't make scaling possible (you can do it on-prem, it just takes months). It makes scaling fast, granular, and reversible — which is the difference 
between "we planned for Black Friday six months ago" and "Black Friday just started, here are 500 more servers in 90 seconds."
Two more points for the exam:
Elasticity. The cloud also scales down automatically when demand drops, so you only pay for what you use. On-prem, you have to buy enough hardware for your busiest day, 
and it sits idle the rest of the time. Security angle. If new servers spin up automatically, each one must come out already secured. That’s where IaC from 3.1 #1 comes
back in: every new server is built from the same secure blueprint.
Why would a company look at patch availability before choosing a system?
Because a system that can't be patched is permanently vulnerable, and you'd rather not buy that problem in.

When you're choosing a system, those are the questions to ask before you sign the contract, not after:
Before choosing a system, ask:

How long will the vendor support it? Check the end-of-life (EOL) date. Once a product reaches end of life, patches stop, however well the hardware still works.
Windows 10 is a recent example. How often are patches released, and how quickly after a flaw is found? A vendor that takes months to fix known vulnerabilities leaves
you exposed. How are patches delivered? Automatic updates are easier than manual ones, which tend to get forgotten (like your medical imaging payroll PC).
Does patching need downtime? That links back to availability. And if a system can’t be patched at all, you need compensating controls, which are other protections 
that make up for it, like segmentation. That’s the term for the exam.
